erts it, as one a reviewer decides without judgement, asking what counts as correct first. Keep the behaviour.\"\n\n  - id: concept_link\n    name: \"Concept Link\"\n    description: \"Every free-text step links to exactly one concept of its own block.\"\n    validation_prompts:\n      - Q: |\n          Link every step of this Item to a product concept of its own block.\n          Given is a state before the behaviour, When an action or event,\n          Then an observable outcome. A concept is one Public interface item\n          and kind of use, as the wording fixes it; an argument's target\n          does not change the concept. Read the product roles and glossary.\n          Score EVERY concept of the step's block, from 0 to 10:\n          10: a plain instance; 9: an instance in other words without doubt;\n          7 or 8: related but partly outside its meaning, or another fits as well;\n          1 to 6: not this concept; 0: unrelated.\n          Read means as CLOSED: it admits only the variations its prose states,\n          never everything in the same area. An unstated variation scores at most 8.\n          A step of the wrong kind for its block scores at most 4 for every\n          concept of that block. Name the other block's concept it is an instance\n          of in other_block_concept, or null if none. For a right-kind step use null.\n          Give a reason for the highest score, or why none fits.\n          judge: clauses are unlinked: omit them from the answer.\n          llm: steps link by their intent; strip the prefix when judging meaning.\n          Answer steps by their supplied keys, with every concept a required\n          key of scores, a reason and other_block_concept. Change nothing.\n        with:\n          concepts: link\n          threshold: 9\n        rationale: \"A step without one closed meaning cannot yield an unambiguous definition.\"\n        docs: [product]\n        F: |\n          Offer the Product Owner a rewritten step applied to the Story or\n          Scenario, a wider concept when its Public interface item and kind of\n          use already exist, or else a new concept for the new item or use.\n          Concepts have concept, means and terms, with variations in closed prose.\n          Apply concept changes only to the product document and a rewrite only\n          to the Item. Show the exact change at the menu and wait for apply.\n\n  - id: concept_bindings\n    name: \"Concept Bindings\"\n    description: \"Every concept linked by this Scenario is bound for its service and unambiguous.\"\n    validation_prompts:\n      - Q: |\n          Judge every supplied linked concept's binding for this Scenario's service.\n          Given its closed means, the binding's does, its references and the\n          complete declared Public interface, is it unambiguous enough for an\n          LLM to write a step definition from? A Given puts the state in place;\n          a When calls; a Then observes and compares. All admitted variations\n          need explicit instructions, inputs and an objective observable result.\n          An intent binding must say the intent an llm: step performs through\n          the UI, with no guessed behaviour or hidden implementation dependency.\n          Answer every binding by its supplied key with unambiguous (boolean)\n          and a reason. Never infer absent instructions or silently pass an\n          ambiguous binding. Change nothing.\n        with:\n          concepts: bindings\n        rationale: \"A linked meaning needs one usable implementation contract for its service.\"\n        docs: [product, architecture_yaml]\n        F: |\n          Offer the System Architect a binding for every missing or ambiguous\n          linked concept, for this Scenario's service: concept, block, service,\n          references and does, or intent for a UI step performed by a model.\n          Judge against the declared Public interface and extend it only where\n          needed. If the meaning needs repair, offer the Product Owner a wider\n          or corrected concept in the product document. Show each exact change\n          at the menu; apply only the approved change after apply.\n\n  - id: concept_words\n    name: \"Concept Words\"\n    description: \"Linked meanings rely only on declared terms and plain English.\"\n    validation_prompts:\n      - Q: |\n          Judge the means of EVERY supplied linked concept, using the product\n          document's glossary for its declared terms. Name every domain word\n          or expression its means relies on that is neither declared in THAT\n          concept's terms nor readable as plain English in that means's context.\n          A glossary entry alone does not declare a term for a concept.\n          Plain English that already defines the word in this context passes;\n          do not demand glossary entries for ordinary words or infer a special\n          domain meaning without evidence. Judge means, not the concept's name,\n          steps, bindings or unrelated concepts. Trust no earlier judgement.\n          Judge each concept independently. Every word in undeclared_words\n          must occur in THAT concept's means; never carry a word from another\n          verdict. Keep the list consistent with the reason: if none is\n          undeclared, return exactly an empty array.\n          For every supplied concept key return undeclared_words (the exact\n          words, or an empty array when none) and a nonempty reason explaining\n          the verdict. Omit no concept. Change nothing.\n        with:\n          concepts: words\n        rationale: \"An undeclared domain word leaves a linked meaning open to interpretation.\"\n        docs: [product]\n        F: |\n          Offer the Product Owner a declared term (a glossary entry with term and\n          means fields, and the word as a string in the affected concept's terms),\n          or reword that concept's means in product word